The horizontal at the top is parallel to the ground, so the angle of depression equals the angle of elevation from the bottom.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (C) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (4,0);
\coordinate (H) at (4.9,2.4);
\draw[dashed] (A) -- (H) node[pos=0.85, above] {horizontal};
\draw (A) -- (C);
\draw (C) -- (B);
\draw[thick] (A) -- (B);
\coordinate (ra) at ($(C)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(C)!0.3cm!(B)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(C)$) -- (rb);
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=1cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = B--A--H};
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.9cm, angle eccentricity=2.1] {angle = A--B--C};
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below left] at (C) {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]

The height is **opposite** the angle, the horizontal distance is **adjacent** to it, and the line of sight is the **hypotenuse**.
[tikz]\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc}
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\coordinate (T) at (4,2.4);
\draw (A) -- (C) node[midway, below] {adjacent};
\draw (C) -- (T) node[midway, right] {opposite};
\draw[thick] (A) -- (T) node[midway, above, sloped] {hypotenuse};
\coordinate (ra) at ($(C)!0.3cm!(A)$);
\coordinate (rb) at ($(C)!0.3cm!(T)$);
\draw (ra) -- ($(ra)+(rb)-(C)$) -- (rb);
\pic[draw, "$\theta$", angle radius=0.9cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = C--A--T};
\end{tikzpicture}[/tikz]
